  "type": "problem",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "description": "<div>\n  <p>Given <code>n</code> pairs of parentheses, return every balanced string that uses exactly <code>n</code> opening and <code>n</code> closing characters. Return the strings in ascending lexicographic order, comparing <code>(</code> before <code>)</code>. Include each balanced arrangement exactly once.</p>\n  <p>For <code>n = 0</code>, the one valid arrangement is the empty string, so return <code>[\"\"]</code>, not an empty array. No other characters may appear. The number of arrangements grows quickly; return the full array for the given bound rather than a count or a lazy iterator.</p>\n  <p>The useful rule while constructing a prefix is that it may contain no more closing than opening characters; otherwise no suffix can repair it. Once a prefix contains the specified number of both kinds, it is complete. Trying an opening character before a closing one naturally yields the required lexicographic order.</p>\n  <p><strong>Example 1:</strong></p>\n  <pre><strong>Input:</strong> n = 3\n<strong>Output:</strong> [\"((()))\",\"(()())\",\"(())()\",\"()(())\",\"()()()\"]</pre>\n  <p><strong>Example 2:</strong></p>\n  <pre><strong>Input:</strong> n = 0\n<strong>Output:</strong> [\"\"]</pre>\n  <p><strong>Example 3:</strong></p>\n  <pre><strong>Input:</strong> n = 2\n<strong>Output:</strong> [\"(())\",\"()()\"]</pre>\n  <p><strong>Constraints:</strong></p>\n  <ul>\n    <li><code>0 &lt;= n &lt;= 8</code>.</li>\n    <li>Every output string has length <code>2 * n</code>.</li>\n    <li>Sort the final array lexicographically if your generation order differs.</li>\n  </ul>\n</div>",
  "templates": {
    "python": "class Solution:\n    def generateParenthesis(self, n: int) -> List[str]:\n        \"\"\"Generate all balanced strings in lexicographic order.\n\n        Args:\n            n: Number of opening and closing parentheses\n\n        Returns:\n            List[str]: Sorted array of valid strings\n        \"\"\"\n        pass",
